From the access-pattern benchmark (code#1344; report: usage-billing-hub goal files/opencode-access-benchmark-2026-10-27.md). Decision: programmatic opencode work defaults to ATTACH — a persistent SSH forward to the laptop background service (127.0.0.1:13420) with raw API control + `opencode run --server` for turns.

Build one small fleet helper (script/library in the pm-stack orbit) that:
- discovers the laptop service (url + password) from the laptop's service-prod.json via laptop-ssh.sh, provisioning the password into a 0600 local secret file — never argv, never logs;
- opens/reuses a multiplexed SSH forward (idempotent; health-checks the local port; reopens on death);
- exposes run/prompt/list operations with the two safety defaults baked in: stdin closed for every `opencode run` (open-pipe stdin hangs the CLI — separate follow-on) and session location passed in the POST body (the ?directory= query param is silently ignored — separate follow-on);
- is the single documented entry point stewardship dispatch + sandbox tooling use for opencode work, so the pattern stops being re-improvised per workstream.
Acceptance: from the sandbox, one command runs a prompt turn against the laptop service end-to-end; killing the forward mid-turn and re-running the helper recovers the view of the still-running session; no secret value appears in any output.

